WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.880
Canada U.S. trade developments are altering supply chain emissions calculations for projects spanning the border. This is Environmental Intelligence, episode 69. Analysis shows the Canada U.S. trade war is affecting supply chain emissions reporting across multiple project types. Waterloo Region researchers advanced safer renewable energy storage methods with direct relevance to grid applications. A transmission link between New England and Canada experienced outages that triggered

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:35.400
a $90 million dispute. If you only note one thing today for your compliance calendar,

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:41.380
reassess emissions inventories for any projects with U.S. component suppliers ahead of the next

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:46.820
quarterly reporting cycle. The Canada U.S. Trade War is hitting supply chain emissions

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:52.920
calculations. Practitioners must adjust scope three emissions estimates when U.S. sourced materials

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:58.860
face new tariffs or routing. This affects federal carbon pricing compliance for any project with

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:04.760
cross-border supply chains. Current models assuming stable North American logistics now require

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:10.940
sensitivity analysis on transport distances and modes. No new federal guidance has been issued

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:16.880
yet on revised calculation methods. The analysis indicates that tariffs are causing rerouting of

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:22.480
shipments and increasing transport emissions in reported inventories. This requires practitioners

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:27.540
to update their models for accuracy in reporting under the greenhouse gas reporting program.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:34.400
Monitor Canada Gazette notices for any updates to reporting templates under the greenhouse gas reporting program.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:39.780
Ontario researchers reported progress on safer renewable energy storage systems.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:45.060
The work targets reduced fire risk and improved performance in grid scale applications.

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:53.260
Practitioners evaluating brownfield redevelopment with on-site renewables should track these technology updates for risk assessment inputs.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:59.080
The findings include specific advances in battery chemistry that lower thermal runaway potential

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.740
during extended operation.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:05.880
This development aligns with provincial goals for renewable integration on contaminated

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:07.780
sites undergoing redevelopment.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:12.500
The event highlights reliability risks for cross-border renewable imports.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:18.220
Canadian practitioners involved in inter-preventual or international energy projects should factor

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:21.800
transmission performance data into environmental impact submissions.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:26.980
The outage occurred when winter demand peaked and exposed vulnerabilities in the clean

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:28.500
energy bridge infrastructure.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:34.240
This case provides concrete data on how transmission failures affect emissions displacement

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:36.340
claims in cross-border projects.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:43.080
Laboratory work converted wood waste into nanocellulose materials effective for dye adsorption and

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:44.580
agricultural applications.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:50.520
Performance data show high surface area and binding capacity suitable for wastewater treatment

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:54.780
contexts. Canadian remediation teams may evaluate this for

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:58.280
institute treatment of organic contaminants where conventional

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:02.660
sorbents are cost prohibitive. The conversion process yields materials

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:06.280
with demonstrated efficacy in binding heavy metals and organic

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:10.260
dyes from aqueous solutions. This approach offers a potential

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:13.820
low cost alternative for site-specific treatment systems where

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:16.800
traditional activated carbon proves uneconomical.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:21.980
A candidate with science background is running for Council in Rosland, British Columbia,

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:24.400
emphasizing environmental priorities.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:30.100
Local land use and contaminated sites decisions could shift depending on election outcomes.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:35.660
Consultants active in the Kootenie region should monitor municipal policy directions after

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:36.080
the vote.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:41.240
The platform includes specific commitments to science-based environmental assessments for

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:42.600
future development proposals.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:47.720
Elections results may influence permitting timelines and review standards for projects

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:48.480
in the area.

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:54.580
A Phase 2 ESA for a logistics facility near the border shows scope three emissions jumping

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:57.800
after a supplier reroutes shipments to avoid new tariffs.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:03.920
The increase stems from longer-truck distances and additional border idling, rather than any

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:05.640
change in the actual materials.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:11.460
Under federal output-based pricing systems, this shifts the facility's compliance obligation

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:14.600
even though the on-site operations state identical.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:19.020
Experienced assessors now build two parallel inventories from the start,

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:21.900
one assuming baseline North American routing,

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:25.000
and one stress tested for trade-driven detours,

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:28.080
then flag the higher number for client disclosure.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:34.560
The mistake I see most often is treating supply chain distances as fixed inputs in the emissions model.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:39.740
The fix is to request supplier logistics declarations at the data collection stage

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:43.120
and rerun the calculation whenever tariff announcements appear.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.580
This adjustment process also requires documenting

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:49.680
the specific tariff measures that triggered each reroute

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:51.580
to maintain audit defensibility.

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:54.820
If you're working on cross-border contaminated sites

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:57.460
or infrastructure under federal carbon pricing,

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.520
review greenhouse gas reporting program templates

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:03.420
for any supply chain assumptions that may need updating.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:06.940
Update internal emissions calculation spreadsheets

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:10.460
to include sensitivity cases for cross-border routing changes.

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:15.800
Monitor Ontario announcements on renewable storage technology approvals that could affect

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:17.320
site redevelopment plans.

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:22.740
Brief clients with U.S. suppliers on potential increases in reported emissions for the next

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:23.780
compliance period.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:28.380
Next federal carbon pricing adjustment review expected within 30 days.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:33.560
Ontario renewable energy storage pilot data release anticipated by month end.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:38.640
And British Columbia Municipal Elections may affect local contaminated sites policies

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:39.900
in several communities.

